        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
This study aimed to determine whether the major reason for high-linear energy transfer (LET) ionizing radiation (IR) to induce excess relative risk (ERR) as compared with low-LET IR on carcinogenesis is because of inefficient Ku-dependent non-homologous end-joining repair. Investigators sought to answer the question whether inefficient Ku-dependent repair is the major reason for high-LET IR to induce ERR on carcinogenesis. 
<p>
High-LET ionizing radiation (IR), when compared with low-LET IR, induces more death both <i>in vitro</i> as well as <i>in vivo</i>, induces more mutations <i>in vitro</i> and induces more cancers <i>in vivo</i>. IR-induced DNA double strand breaks (DSBs) are the severest threat for IR-induced killing as well as are the initial and the essential event for IR-induced genomic integrity. Two kinds of major DSB repair exist in eukaryotic cells, homologous recombination repair (HRR) and non-homologous end-joining (NHEJ). HRR is the major pathway for repairing DSBs in yeast, but both the NHEJ and HRR pathways are required for DSB repair in mammalian cells. NHEJ repair includes a Ku-dependent main pathway and a PARP-1 dependent complementary pathway. High-LET IR induces more cell killing when compared with low LET IR and is described as relative biologic effectiveness (RBE), which is believed to be caused by ineffective rejoining of DNA DSBs involved in the Ku-dependent NHEJ pathway. However, it remains unclear whether high-LET IR also affects the pathways of HRR and PARP-1-dependent complementary NHEJ. Recent data suggest that high-LET IR affects only the Ku-dependent main NHEJ but not other repairs, including HRR and PARP-1-dependent complementary NHEJ. Based on the data, investigators hypothesized that the major reason for high LET IR to induce excess relative risk on carcinogenesis is because of inefficient Ku-dependent NHEJ repair.
The purpose of this experiment was to test the following three specific aims:<ol><br>
<li>Determine whether the major reason for high-LET IR inducing excess deaths in cells and in mice is because of inefficient Ku-dependent NHEJ repair. 
<li>Determine whether the major reason for high-LET IR inducing ERR on carcinogenesis in mice is because of inefficient Ku-dependent NHEJ repair. 
<li>Determine whether the major reason for high-LET IR inducing ERR on genomic instability is because of inefficient Ku-dependent NHEJ repair.</ol>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
To test their hypothesis that the major reason for high-LET IR inducing excess death in mammals is because of inefficient Ku-dependent NHEJ repair, investigators compared high-LET IR and low-LET IR-induced death of different repair deficient mice at one month following whole body radiation exposure after a single dose. They also compared high-LET IR and low-LET IR-induced carcinogenesis frequencies in different repair deficient mice at six months to a year following whole body exposure to single or fractionation dose of high-LET or low-LET IR. In addition, investigators compared the effects of the repair pathways (NHEJ or HRR) on the low dose-induced cell protective response and the protective role of checkpoint activation in protecting cells from high-LET or low-LET radiation induced killing by using different repair deficient cell lines.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
<i>Cell work: Investigators carried out the experiments by using different types of cells and obtained the following results:</i><ol>
<li>They elucidated why S-phase cells are more sensitive to heavy ion, the high LET radiation, although they are more resistant to x or gamma rays. After further studying the mechanism, investigators believe that the phenotype of S-phase sensitive to high-LET radiation is due to the fact that high-LET radiation could generate much smaller fragments that result in inefficient Ku binding in S-phase, which further supports their hypothesis that high-LET killing more cells when compared with low-LET radiation is because of the inhibition of Ku-dependent NHEJ.
<li>They demonstrated that the checkpoint response plays a more protective role in HZE particle-irradiated cells than in X ray-irradiated cells. Investigators previously reported that the DNA double strand break (DSB)-activated checkpoint mainly facilitates HRR and has little effect on NHEJ. 
<li>They indicated that low dose radiation-induced adaptive responses are through promoting NHEJ and explain that low dose radiation could not induce any protective role in high-LET irradiated cells. It has been known for more than two decades that low dose radiation (< 0.1 Gy) could induce adaptive responses. The adaptive responses could protect cells and animals from high-challenge dose-induced killing and carcinogenesis. Because DNA DSBs induced by radiation are the severest threat for cell survival and genetic integrity, the low dose adaptive responses are believed to promote DNA repair. However, it remains unclear which DNA DSB repair pathway, NHEJ or HRR, is promoted by the low dose radiation. Investigators found that low dose radiation could not induce any protective role in high-LET irradiated cells.
<li>They show for the first time that microRNA-21 (miR-21) plays an important role in HZE particle-induced carcinogenesis. By obtaining the paraffin-embedded liver tissues from irradiated mice, investigators found that miR-21 was much higher expressed in liver tumors than in normal liver tissues and further found that ionizing radiation, especially high LET radiation could stimulate miR-21 expression. Investigators further demonstrated that after the non-irradiated, low-LET or high-LET irradiated human hepatocytes were over-expressed with miR-21, these cells became tumorigenesis in nude mice. The tumors derived from high-LET-irradiated-cells were largest, and accompanied by more significant changes in the miR-21-targets: PTEN and RECK. In addition, investigators found that IR-induced up-regulation of miR-21 depended on the up-regulation/activation of AP-1 and the ErbB/Stat3 pathway, which was also IR dose dependent. Taken together, investigators concluded that IR-induced up-regulation of miR-21 plays an important role in IR, especially high-LET IR-promoted liver carcinogenesis.</ol>
<p>
<i>Animal work: Investigators carried out the experiments by using mice with different genetic backgrounds and obtained the following results:</i><ol>
<li>Acute experiments: They discovered that more effects of high-LET radiation than low-LET radiation-induced killing is due to the inefficient NHEJ repair, which is involved in Mre11 and Ku responding differently to shorter DNA fragments in vitro and to the DNA from high-LET irradiated cells in vivo. These findings provide strong evidence that the different DNA DSB binding properties of Mre11 and Ku determine the different efficiencies of HRR and NHEJ to repair high-LET radiation-induced DSBs.
<li>Chronic experiments: They elucidated that DNA repair plays an important role in protecting mice from HZE particle-induced carcinogenesis. The results showed that ionizing radiation increased tumorigenesis, especial lung tumorigenesis. These phenotypes were more clearly shown in DNA repair (HRR or NHEJ) deficient mice. However, they did not see dramatically different frequencies of carcinogenesis between high-LET and low-LET irradiated mice although the tumorigenesis frequency in DNA-PKcs-/- (NHEJ deficient) mice is higher than in Rad54-/- (HRR deficient) mice and the tumorigenesis frequency in Rad54-/- mice is higher than in wild type mice. These results indicate that both NHEJ and HRR protect mice from radiation-induced carcinogenesis but besides DNA repair, other factors also contribute to radiation-induced carcinogenesis. Although acute high-LET radiation-induced more killings then low-LET radiation, high-LET radiation does not show a dramatically higher carcinogenesis frequency than low-LET radiation. In addition, except high-LET fractionation radiation (0.2 Gy x5) that showed higher liver carcinogenesis than high-LET single dose (1 Gy) irradiated in DNA-PKcs-/- mice, fractionation radiation (0.2 Gy x5) did not show a dramatically different frequency for other types of mouse tumors when compared with single dose (1 Gy) irradiation. </ol>
<p>
        </div>

